---
// "Server mode. Host it anywhere." The remote-dux pitch.
//
// PLACEMENT: directly after InstallSection, before the blog/contributors tail.
// The install section is where the reader learns that ONE binary carries both
// front ends and that `dux server` comes out of that same executable. This
// section is the immediate "so put that binary on a box that is not your
// laptop" follow-through, and it is the only section that asks the reader to
// think about deployment, so it belongs after the thing it deploys rather than
// interrupting the feature narrative above.
//
// THE SPINE IS THE MISSING LOGIN, NOT A FOOTNOTE.
//
// dux has no authentication of any kind. Not a weak one, not a default
// password: there is no auth layer. Every client that reaches the port shares
// one workspace and can drive any agent, browse the server's filesystem through
// the project picker, and read every session. So "host it anywhere" is only
// true as "host it behind something that authenticates", and that sentence is
// the section's second paragraph rather than a warning strip at the bottom.
//
// It is also, deliberately, part of the pitch. A competent reader deciding
// whether to put this on a VPS is better served by knowing exactly what they
// are signing up for than by a vague reassurance, and the honest version
// happens to be the more attractive one: the proxy is one Compose file and the
// docs page has it.
//
// The two built-in guards (the Host allowlist and the same-origin check) get
// their own card SAYING THEY ARE NOT ACCESS CONTROL. They stop a browser being
// tricked into talking to your dux; they do nothing about a person who visits
// the URL. Leaving them unmentioned invites the reader to discover them and
// mistake them for a gate, so the section names them and then disowns them.
//
// The topology block is `<pre>` inside an `overflow-x-auto` wrapper: it is
// about 66 columns wide, so it fits most phones outright and scrolls in its own
// box rather than the page body where it does not.
---

<section id="server-mode" class="anchor-offset py-16 sm:py-24">
  <div class="container-app">
    {/* Full width: nothing sits to the right of this intro, so a cap would just leave a hole there. */}
    <div>
      <span class="pill">▸ server mode</span>
      <h2 class="heading mt-3 text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight sm:text-4xl">
        Server mode. Host it <span class="text-[color:var(--color-accent)]">anywhere</span>.
      </h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[color:var(--color-muted)] leading-relaxed">
        <span class="mono text-[color:var(--color-text)]">dux server</span>{" "}
        is the same binary you just installed, serving the web UI instead of
        drawing a terminal. Which means the box it runs on does not have to be
        the box you are sitting at. Put it on a VPS, install your favourite
        agent CLIs and <span class="mono text-[color:var(--color-text)]">git</span>{" "}
        (plus <span class="mono text-[color:var(--color-text)]">gh</span> if you
        want the GitHub bits), and your agents run on a machine with a real
        uplink that does not go to sleep when you close the lid.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[color:var(--color-muted)] leading-relaxed">
        Then comes the part everyone skips, so here it is first:{" "}
        <strong class="text-[color:var(--color-text)]">dux has no login.</strong>{" "}
        None. Not a weak one, not a default password, not a token you forgot to
        rotate. There is no authentication layer in dux at all, and none is
        planned, because the job belongs to the thing in front of it. So
        "host it anywhere" is really "host it behind something that
        authenticates you, over HTTPS". Do that and this is a genuinely lovely
        way to work. Skip it and you have published a shell.
      </p>
    </div>

    {/* The diagram is real elements, not a picture of one. ASCII box drawing
        reads as a terminal, and this is the one part of the page that is not
        about a terminal: it is a deployment. It also could not reflow, so on a
        phone it either scrolled sideways or shrank into nothing.

        Laid out as a row that wraps: on a phone the three stages stack and the
        connectors turn to point down. */}
    <div class="mt-10 rounded-xl border border-[color:var(--color-border)] bg-[color:var(--color-panel)]/40 p-5 backdrop-blur sm:p-6">
      <div class="flex flex-col items-stretch gap-3 lg:flex-row lg:items-center">
        <div class="flex-1 rounded-lg border border-[color:var(--color-border-soft)] bg-[color:var(--color-ink)] p-4">
          <div class="mono text-[10px] uppercase tracking-wider text-[color:var(--color-dim)]">you, anywhere</div>
          <div class="mt-1.5 text-sm font-medium text-[color:var(--color-text)]">A browser, or a phone</div>
          <p class="mt-1 text-xs text-[color:var(--color-muted)]">Nothing installed. The workspace is wherever dux is.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="flex items-center justify-center gap-2 text-[color:var(--color-dim)] lg:flex-col">
          <span class="mono text-[10px] uppercase tracking-wider">https</span>
          <span aria-hidden="true" class="text-[color:var(--color-accent)] lg:rotate-0">
            <span class="lg:hidden">↓</span><span class="hidden lg:inline">→</span>
          </span>
        </div>

        <div class="flex-[1.4] rounded-lg border border-[color:var(--color-accent)]/40 bg-[color:var(--color-ink)] p-4">
          <div class="mono text-[10px] uppercase tracking-wider text-[color:var(--color-dim)]">a box that is not your laptop</div>
          <div class="mt-1.5 text-sm font-medium text-[color:var(--color-text)]">Reverse proxy, TLS, and a login</div>
          <p class="mt-1 text-xs text-[color:var(--color-muted)]">
            oauth2-proxy decides who you are. Everything past this point has already been let in.
          </p>

          <div class="mt-3 flex items-center gap-2 text-[color:var(--color-dim)]">
            <span aria-hidden="true" class="text-[color:var(--color-accent)]">↓</span>
            <span class="mono text-[10px] uppercase tracking-wider">http, loopback only</span>
          </div>

          <div class="mt-2 rounded-lg border border-[color:var(--color-border-soft)] bg-[color:var(--color-panel-2)]/60 p-3">
            <div class="mono text-xs font-medium text-[color:var(--color-text)]">dux server</div>
            <div class="mt-1.5 flex flex-wrap gap-1.5">
              <span class="pill pill-xs">claude</span>
              <span class="pill pill-xs">codex</span>
              <span class="pill pill-xs">git</span>
              <span class="pill pill-xs">gh</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

      <div class="mt-6 grid items-start gap-4 md:grid-cols-3">
        <div class="card p-4">
          <div class="flex items-center gap-2">
            <span class="shrink-0 text-[color:var(--color-warning)]">▲</span>
            <strong class="text-sm text-[color:var(--color-text)]">
              The two built-in guards are not a login
            </strong>
          </div>
          <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-[color:var(--color-muted)]">
            dux does run a Host-header allowlist and a same-origin check, and
            they are worth having: they stop a hostile web page tricking{" "}
            <em>your</em> browser into driving your server. They do nothing
            whatsoever about a person who simply opens the URL. Anti-rebinding
            and anti-CSRF, not access control. Do not confuse the two.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="card p-4">
          <div class="flex items-center gap-2">
            <span class="shrink-0 text-[color:var(--color-accent)]">✦</span>
            <strong class="text-sm text-[color:var(--color-text)]">
              One workspace, shared by everyone who reaches it
            </strong>
          </div>
          <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-[color:var(--color-muted)]">
            There are no user accounts, so there is nothing to scope anything
            to. Whoever gets in can attach to any agent or terminal, type into a
            session someone else is using, run git, and browse the server's
            filesystem through the project picker. That is deliberate for a
            per-developer tool. It is also exactly why the gate goes in front.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="card p-4">
          <div class="flex items-center gap-2">
            <span class="shrink-0 text-[color:var(--color-pink)]">✦</span>
            <strong class="text-sm text-[color:var(--color-text)]">
              Two things any proxy in front must get right
            </strong>
          </div>
          <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-[color:var(--color-muted)]">
            It has to pass <strong class="text-[color:var(--color-text)]">WebSockets</strong>{" "}
            through, because every terminal rides one and so does the change
            feed that tells the page when anything moved, and it has to forward
            the original{" "}
            <span class="mono text-[color:var(--color-text)]">Host</span>, or
            the allowlist answers <span class="mono">403</span>. Rewriting{" "}
            <span class="mono text-[color:var(--color-text)]">Host</span> to
            loopback is the sneaky one: it passes the allowlist and then fails
            every upgrade and every write on the origin check.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

    <div class="mt-6 grid items-stretch gap-2.5 md:grid-cols-2">
      <div class="flex items-start gap-2.5 rounded-lg border border-[color:var(--color-border-soft)] bg-[color:var(--color-panel-2)]/50 px-3.5 py-2.5 text-sm text-[color:var(--color-muted)]">
        <span class="mt-0.5 shrink-0 text-[color:var(--color-success)]">✓</span>
        <p>
          <strong class="text-[color:var(--color-text)]">Your phone is a real client, not a consolation prize.</strong>{" "}
          Below tablet width dux draws a purpose-built shell: a hub of projects
          and sessions, a full-screen terminal, and above the soft keyboard an
          accessory bar with the keys a phone does not have. Below that, a
          compose box, so autocorrect, swipe typing and voice input finally have
          a buffer to work on instead of firing straight into a PTY.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="flex items-start gap-2.5 rounded-lg border border-[color:var(--color-border-soft)] bg-[color:var(--color-panel-2)]/50 px-3.5 py-2.5 text-sm text-[color:var(--color-muted)]">
        <span class="mt-0.5 shrink-0 text-[color:var(--color-success)]">✓</span>
        <p>
          <strong class="text-[color:var(--color-text)]">Staying on your own network is easier still.</strong>{" "}
          If you do not need the open internet, do not take it. dux binds your
          Tailscale address out of the box, which is a private path to the same
          workspace with nothing to configure and nothing to publish.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-6 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
      <a href="/docs/public-hosting" class="btn btn-primary">
        Put it behind oauth2-proxy →
      </a>
      <a href="/docs/server-mode" class="btn btn-ghost">
        Server mode overview
      </a>
      <a href="/docs/tailscale" class="btn btn-ghost">
        Reach it over Tailscale
      </a>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
